The !!Ptolemaic Decrees!! were a series of decrees by [[ synods | synod ]] of
[[ ancient Egyptian priests | ancient-egyptian-religion ]]. They were issued
in the [[ Ptolemaic Kingdom ]], which controlled [[ Egypt | ancient-egypt ]]
from 305 BC to 30 BC. In each decree, the benefactions of the reigning
[[ pharaoh ]], especially towards the priesthood, are recognised, and
religious honours are decreed for him.[[ ^1 ]]

Two decrees were issued under [[ Ptolemy III Euergetes ]] (the Decree of
Alexandria and [[ Decree of Canopus ]]), another under
[[ Ptolemy IV Philopator ]] (the [[ Raphia Decree ]]), and others under
[[ Ptolemy V Epiphanes ]] (the [[ Decree of Memphis | rosetta-stone-decree ]]
and the two Philensis Decrees). Multiple copies of the decrees, inscribed on
stone [[ steles | stele ]], were erected in temple
[[ courtyards | courtyard ]], as specified in the text of the decrees.[[ ^2 ]]

There exist three copies plus a fragment of the Decree of Canopus, two copies
of the Memphis Decree (one imperfect), and two and a half copies of the text
of the [[ Rosetta Stone ]], including the copy on the [[ Nubayrah Stele ]] and
a temple wall inscription with edits, or scene replacements, completed by
subsequent scribes.

== 243 BC Decree of Alexandria (Ptolemy III)

The Decree of Alexandria was issued on the 13th of Gorpiaios (3 December) 243
BCE. It proclaimed that statues of Ptolemy III and his wife [[ Berenice II ]]
as well as a shrine for them should be set up in each temple to worship them
as beneficent gods.[[ ^3 ]] The reasons cited for these honours include the
king's services to Egypt in general, but above all his support for the
temples; it is therefore possible that the decree was intended to strike a
balance between the Greek monarch and the still powerful priesthoods.[[ ^4 ]]

== 238 BC Decree of Canopus (Ptolemy III)

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Kanopos_Kararnamesi.jpg/250px-Kanopos_Kararnamesi.jpg" height="333" width="250"

  <figcaption>Decree of Canopus

The Decree was issued on 7 Appellaios (Mac.) = 17 Tybi (Eg.) year 9 of Ptolemy
III = Thursday 7 March 238 BCE (proleptic Julian calendar).

- Stone 1: !!Stele of Canopus!!, (no. 1), found 1866, 37 lines of hieroglyphs, 74 lines of [[ Demotic | demotic-egyptian ]] (right side), 76 lines of Greek [[ 'capitals' | majuscule ]], fine limestone.
- Stone 2: !![[ Stele of Canopus | decree-of-canopus ]]!! (no. 2), found 1881, 26 lines hieroglyphs, 20 lines Demotic, 64 lines Greek 'capitals', white limestone.
- 3rd partial text with lines of hieroglyphs (now in the [[ Louvre ]]).
- 4th text was discovered in 2004 at [[ Bubastis ]], by the German-Egyptian 'Tell Basta Project'.

== 217 BC Raphia Decree (Ptolemy IV)

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Tell_el-Maskhutah_Raphia_Decree_Stele.jpg/250px-Tell_el-Maskhutah_Raphia_Decree_Stele.jpg" height="256" width="250"

  <figcaption>The Raphia decree, found at Memphis.

- Stone 1: !!Stele of Memphis!! (CGC 31088), found in 1902 at the site of ancient Memphis, hieroglyphs, Demotic, and Greek, dark granite.
- Stone 2: !!Pithom Stele, No. II!! (CGC 50048), found in 1923 at [[ Tell el-Maskhuta ]], hieroglyphs (front), 42 lines Demotic (back), virtually complete, providing an almost total translation, and Greek (side), sandstone.
- Stone 3: !!Fragment from Tod!! found between 1934-36) disappearing shortly after discover, published based only on photographic evidence[[ ^5 ]]

== 196 BC Decree of Memphis (Ptolemy V)

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/RosettaStoneDetail.jpg/250px-RosettaStoneDetail.jpg" height="189" width="250"

  <figcaption>Rosetta Stone detail

- Stone 1: !!The [[ Rosetta Stone ]]!!, found 1799, (remaining) hieroglyphs, 14 lines, 32 lines Demotic, 54 lines Greek [[ 'capitals' | majuscule ]], dark granite ([[ granodiorite ]]).
- Stone 2: !![[ Nubayrah Stele ]]!!, found in the early 1880s, hieroglyphs, lines 1–27 were used to complete the missing lines on the Rosetta Stone, Demotic, Greek capitals, limestone.
- Site 3: the Temple of [[ Philae ]], inscribed hieroglyphs from the Third Decree on walls, also overwritten, with scenes and figures of humans/gods.

== 186 BC Philensis II Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued at Alexandria on 6 September 186 BC after the suppression of
[[ Ankhwennefer's | ankhwennefer-pharaoh ]] Upper Egyptian
revolt.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^6 ]]

- Stone 1: //Stele of Philae II//
- Stone 2: //Stele of Cairo//

== 186 BC New Year's Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued on 9 October 186 BC (New Year's day), perhaps to celebrate the birth of
[[ Ptolemy VI ]].[[ ^6 ]]

- Papyrus 1: //P. Kroll. = P. Köln// 7.313

== 185 BC Philensis I Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued at Memphis on 29 October 185 BC, upon the enthronement of an
[[ Apis bull ]].[[ ^2 ]][[ ^6 ]]

- Stone 1: //Stele of Philae I//

== 182 BC Memphis decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued on 29 May 182 BC, after a visit to Memphis by the
[[ Mnevis bull ]].[[ ^6 ]]

== 161 BC Decree (Ptolemy VI)

Issued on 31 July 161 BC, upon a royal visit to Memphis, shortly after
[[ Ptolemy VI ]] was restored to power.[[ ^6 ]]

- Stone 1: Cairo Stele 22184.

